    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S478A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in\nrelation to requiring an educational awareness campaign relative to\nthe dangers of acting as a pedestrian while distracted by using a\nmobile device\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo require that the New York City Department of Transportation develop\na Citywide public education campaign relative to the dangers of acting\nas a pedestrian while distracted by texting, or other uses of a mobile\ndevice. As part of this campaign, an emphasis shall be placed upon the\nparticular harms inherent in operating a mobile device while crossing\nan intersection and/or walking in or near a street.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: adds a new Section 19-193 to the New York City\nAdministrative Code.\n\nSection 2: sets forth the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nIn recent years, there has been a marked increase in traffic accidents\ncaused by operators of motor vehicles who are distracted by texting,\nor otherwise using mobile phones. According to Distraction.gov, the\nfederal government's website aimed at preventing distracted driving,\n3,154 people were killed in motor vehicle crashes involving distracted\ndrivers. To combat this problem, Governor Cuomo and The Legislature\nhave taken acting to ban texting while driving in New York, as well as\nestablishing 'Text Stops' along the State's highways to provide a safe\nlocation for drivers to communicate using their mobile devices.\n\nAs distracted driving has led to an increase in injuries and\nfatalities, it so too is the case that pedestrians are often engaged\nin texting and other uses of their mobile devices while crossing busy\nintersections and other thoroughfares. In an August 2015 report by the\nGovernors Highway Safety Association, national pedestrian fatalities\nincreased by 15 percent from 2009 to 2013 with 4,735 deaths in 2013\nalone. Additionally, the percentage of pedestrians killed while using\ncell phones has risen, from less than 1 percent in 2004 to more than\n3.5 percent in 2010. All signs point unfortunately point to an\nincreasing trend.\n\nIn the especially busy streets across all five boroughs of New York\nCity, 269 traffic fatalities were recorded in 2014, in addition to\nthousands of injuries stemming from traffic accidents. In this perfect\nstorm of dangerous traffic conditions and an increase in unsafe,\ndistracted walking, it is appropriate for the City Department of\nTransportation to engage in a public education campaign to bring\nwidespread attention to this important issue.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015-2015-S6266\n\n FISCAL IMPACT :\n\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
